  {
   "n": 6,
   "type": "MCQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0.66",
   "html": "<p>\n\tWhich of the following statement is false?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "<p>\n\t<em>L</em>(<em>L</em>, * ⊕) be a lattice and <em>S</em> ⊆ <em>L</em> be a subset of <em>L</em>. The algebra (<em>S</em>, *, ⊕) is a sub lattice of <em>L</em>(<em>L</em>, * ⊕) if and only if <em>S</em> is closed under both operation * and ⊕.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "Every chain is distributive lattice."
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "<p>\n\tIf a lattice said to be complementary lattice then every element of <em>L</em> has only one complement.</p>"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "A lattice is called complete if each of it’s non-empty subset has least upper bound and a great lower bound."
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "C"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "C",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 2,
    "neg": 0.66
   },
   "solution": "<p>\n\t(c)<br />\n\tComplementary lattice may not have unique complement for every element.</p>"
  },
  {
   "n": 7,
   "type": "MSQ",
   "section": "Technical",
   "tags": "Technical • COMPUTER SCIENCE • +2 / -0",
   "html": "<p>\n\tThe degree sequence of a simple graph is the sequence of the degree of the nodes in the graph in decreasing order. Which of the following sequence can be the degree sequence of any graph?</p>",
   "options": [
    {
     "key": "A",
     "html": "7, 6, 5, 4, 4, 3, 2, 1"
    },
    {
     "key": "B",
     "html": "7, 6, 6, 4, 4, 3, 2, 2"
    },
    {
     "key": "C",
     "html": "6, 6, 6, 6, 3, 3, 2, 2"
    },
    {
     "key": "D",
     "html": "None of these"
    }
   ],
   "answer": [
    "A",
    "B"
   ],
   "natRange": null,
   "answerText": "A, B",
   "marks": {
    "pos": 2,
    "neg": 0
   },
   "solution": "<p>\n\t(a, b)<br />\n\tUsing Havell-Hakimi algorithm:<br />\n\t(i) Remove top node of the sequence.<br />\n\t(ii) Substract 1 from as many nodes in remaining sequence as the degree of top node that was removed.<br />\n\t(iii) Rearrange the sequence in non-increasing order.<br />\n\t(iv) Check if result sequence is a graph.<br />\n\t(v) Proceed again step (i)<br />\n\tIf the given sequence is not a graph. We will see a violation in step (iv). Such as presence of negative degrees in the sequence. Otherwise the algorithm will bottom out with a degree sequence consisting of only even number of 1&rsquo;s and any number of 0&rsquo;s.</p>"
  },
